{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-102945" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "What surface is defined by the equation $ϕ=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ in spherical polar coordinates?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069421/state.json" "file_sha256" "5d907c6218bcd463461354f843f268ecba8f3dd3841c782abd43d07eaf86216b"} {"question" "q-102945" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We write the position of a point (vector) in spherical polar coordinates as $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ),$ where \n\n- $\\rho \\ge 0$ is the distance from the point to the origin,\n\n- $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ is the angle between the point and the positive $x$-axis, and\n\n- $ϕ\\in [0,\\pi ]$ is the angle the corresponding vector makes with the positive $z$-axis.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our equation:\n\n$$\nϕ=\\frac{\\pi }{4}\n$$\n\nThis equation specifies that for any point on the surface, the angle that the corresponding vector makes with the positive $z$-axis must equal $\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ On the other hand, $\\rho $ and $\\theta $ can vary arbitrarily in their respective domains. \n\nTherefore, since $\\rho \\ge 0,$ our surface is a half-cone.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069421/assets/27f9b580436d8e1aebeb4f150643a22266e9ad7f488b98e63f8a49dd4f1aa97f.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069421/state.json" "file_sha256" "5d907c6218bcd463461354f843f268ecba8f3dd3841c782abd43d07eaf86216b"} {"question" "q-102945" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069421/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5549aa7705bd3391e8dee2b14e54106454742a21bc1953b59ba2e925e0899ef7"} {"question" "q-102945" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069421/state.json" "file_sha256" "5d907c6218bcd463461354f843f268ecba8f3dd3841c782abd43d07eaf86216b"} {"question" "q-102945" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "A circular cylinder"] ["text" "A half-cone"] ["text" "A half-plane that passes through the $z$-axis"] ["text" "A plane that is parallel to the $xy$-plane"] ["text" "A sphere"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069421/state.json" "file_sha256" "5d907c6218bcd463461354f843f268ecba8f3dd3841c782abd43d07eaf86216b"} {"question" "q-102945" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "A half-cone"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069421/state.json" "file_sha256" "5d907c6218bcd463461354f843f268ecba8f3dd3841c782abd43d07eaf86216b"} {"question" "q-135302" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "What surface is defined by the equation $\\theta =\\frac{4\\pi }{3}$ in spherical polar coordinates?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069421/state.json" "file_sha256" "5d907c6218bcd463461354f843f268ecba8f3dd3841c782abd43d07eaf86216b"} {"question" "q-135302" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We write the position of a point (vector) in spherical polar coordinates as $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ),$ where \n\n- $\\rho \\ge 0$ is the distance from the point to the origin,\n\n- $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ is the angle between the point and the positive $x$-axis, and\n\n- $ϕ\\in [0,\\pi ]$ is the angle the corresponding vector makes with the positive $z$-axis.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our equation:\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\frac{4\\pi }{3}\n$$\n\nThis equation specifies that for any point on the surface, the angle that the corresponding vector makes with the positive $x$-axis must equal $\\frac{4\\pi }{3}.$ On the other hand, $\\rho $ and $ϕ$ can vary arbitrarily in their respective domains. \n\nTherefore, since $\\rho \\ge 0,$ our surface is a half-plane.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069421/assets/0043ffdc885e7edeebb7c4df571466fe4f13dfb08728290cd6d54f47777a7179.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069421/state.json" "file_sha256" "5d907c6218bcd463461354f843f268ecba8f3dd3841c782abd43d07eaf86216b"} {"question" "q-135302" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069421/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5549aa7705bd3391e8dee2b14e54106454742a21bc1953b59ba2e925e0899ef7"} {"question" "q-135302" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069421/state.json" "file_sha256" "5d907c6218bcd463461354f843f268ecba8f3dd3841c782abd43d07eaf86216b"} {"question" "q-135302" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "A circular cylinder"] ["text" "A half-line"] ["text" "A half-plane that passes through the $x$-axis"] ["text" "A half-plane that passes through the $z$-axis"] ["text" "A paraboloid"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069421/state.json" "file_sha256" "5d907c6218bcd463461354f843f268ecba8f3dd3841c782abd43d07eaf86216b"} {"question" "q-135302" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "A half-plane that passes through the $z$-axis"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069421/state.json" "file_sha256" "5d907c6218bcd463461354f843f268ecba8f3dd3841c782abd43d07eaf86216b"} {"question" "q-161803" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069421/assets/f8a15e2452febed245c0e0bc8a0e7cad8a3579f7205ec4a295fc36875b0d7141.png)\n\nThe solid enclosed by the hemisphere ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=16$ for $z\\ge 0,$ shown above, can be described in spherical polar coordinates as" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069421/state.json" "file_sha256" "5d907c6218bcd463461354f843f268ecba8f3dd3841c782abd43d07eaf86216b"} {"question" "q-161803" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We write the position of a point (vector) in spherical polar coordinates as $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ),$ where \n\n- $\\rho \\ge 0$ is the distance from the point to the origin,\n\n- $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ is the angle between the point and the positive $x$-axis, and\n\n- $ϕ\\in [0,\\pi ]$ is the angle the corresponding vector makes with the positive $z$-axis.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069421/assets/85ebe508035ae538d6becd73e9563be903a8cfcc4764550e131c35c84f7b80de.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that inside our region, the spherical polar coordinates vary within the following domains:  \n\n- $\\rho \\in [0,4]$\n\n- $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ]$\n\n- $ϕ\\in [0,\\pi /2]$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 4,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi /2}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069421/state.json" "file_sha256" "5d907c6218bcd463461354f843f268ecba8f3dd3841c782abd43d07eaf86216b"} {"question" "q-161803" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069421/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5549aa7705bd3391e8dee2b14e54106454742a21bc1953b59ba2e925e0899ef7"} {"question" "q-161803" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069421/state.json" "file_sha256" "5d907c6218bcd463461354f843f268ecba8f3dd3841c782abd43d07eaf86216b"} {"question" "q-161803" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 2,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi }"] ["math" "{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 2,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le 2\\pi }"] ["math" "{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 4,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi /2}"] ["math" "{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 4,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi }"] ["math" "{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 4,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi /2,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi }"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069421/state.json" "file_sha256" "5d907c6218bcd463461354f843f268ecba8f3dd3841c782abd43d07eaf86216b"} {"question" "q-161803" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/c